# Lesson 3, new exercise: set up dummy's clubs by forcing out the ace, then throw diamond losers on them.
title: Clubs for your diamonds
id: L03-19
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3
student: S
board: 51
dealer: S
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  Q742   K4     753    KQJ9
  E  T3     8652   QJT6   T54
  S  AKJ85  A73    K42    62
  W  96     QJT9   A98    A873

auction:
  S 1S*    = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 15 high-card points and five spades: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3S     = 4+ spades, an opening hand or better: game forcing
  E Pass
  S 4S*    = Game
     key: Partner's jump raise forces to game. Bid it.
     not Pass: Partner's 3♠ forces to game. Did you add your points to partner's opening hand?
     not *: Partner showed an opening hand or better and four spades. Can you see 25?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner's jump to 3♠ showed four spades and an opening hand or
  better, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades first,
  and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 3
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 0 = The third heart, but dummy can ruff it.
  diamonds: 2 = Two after the ace. Both may go away on dummy's clubs.
  clubs: 1 = The ace. Force it out to set up dummy's clubs.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Dummy has four clubs to your two. What happens once the ♣A is gone?
play:
  1   W HQ  N H4  E H2  S HA
      note: Four losers: one heart, two diamonds and the ♣A. Once the ♣A is gone, dummy's clubs can take care of your diamonds. Draw trumps first.
  2   S S5  W S6  N SQ  E S3
      note: Draw trumps. Start with dummy's queen, the high card from the short side, so that you finish in your own hand.
  3   N S2  E ST  S SA  W S9
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Trumps are drawn.
  4   S C2  W C3  N CK  E C4
      note: Now force out the ♣A.
  5   N CQ  E C5  S C6  W CA
      note: Keep going until the ace is gone.
  6   W HJ  N HK  E H5  S H3
  7   N CJ  E CT  S D2  W C7
      note: Dummy's clubs are winners now. Throw away your diamond losers.
  8   N C9  E D6  S D4  W C8
      note: Another discard: your last diamond loser goes away.
  9   N D3  E DT  S DK  W DA
      note: Now the last diamond loser. West wins the ace: a trick you counted.
  10  W HT  N S7  E H6  S H7
      note: Dummy has no hearts left, so it ruffs.
  11  N D5  E DQ  S S8  W D9
      note: Dummy's last diamond. You ruff it.
  12  S SJ  W H9  N S4  E H8
      note: Your last trumps are winners.
  13  S SK  W D8  N D7  E DJ
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps, force out the ♣A, and throw two diamond losers on
  dummy's clubs. Dummy's last trump took care of the heart.

  Notice that forcing out the ace is the same idea you use at no-trump: you
  lose one trick to set up several.
